 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "SHANDON BELLS. The tower of St Anne's has eight bells you can ring yourself, and a clock with four faces that for years each told a different time. Corkonians call it 'the four-faced liar'. They say it with love.",
   "x": 25.0,
   "y": 10.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE ENGLISH MARKET. A covered market since 1788, with fish, fruit, spices and the best tripe in Ireland, if you like that sort of thing. Queen Elizabeth II stopped to chat to a fishmonger here in 2011, and the fish said nothing.",
   "x": 34.0,
   "y": 27.0,
   "local": {
    "ga": "An Margadh Sasanach"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "CORK. In Irish, Corcaigh, 'the marsh'. The city is built on an island in the River Lee. Corkonians say Cork is the real capital of Ireland, and have said so since 1920 at least.",
   "x": 11.0,
   "y": 22.0,
   "local": {
    "ga": "Corcaigh"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "THE LEE. It splits in two to flow round the city, then joins up again to head for Cork Harbour. It's the sort of river that's a bit of a tease.",
   "x": 13.0,
   "y": 38.0,
   "local": {
    "ga": "An Laoi"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "ST FIN BARRE'S. A neo-Gothic cathedral from 1879 with three spires, a golden angel and stone beasts on the roof, on the spot where the city's patron saint founded a monastery in the 7th century.",
   "x": 33.0,
   "y": 38.0
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 1,
   "text": "Road to Blarney closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 1,
   "text": "Road to Blarney closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 1,
   "text": "Road to Blarney closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 50,
   "text": "Road to Kinsale closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: The English Market has traded on this spot since 1788.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "cork_english_market",
   "title": "English Market, 1788"
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 2,
   "plaque": "cork_blarney_stone",
   "title": "The Blarney Stone",
   "text": "A few miles north-west at Blarney Castle, built by the MacCarthys, is a stone set high in the battlements. Legend says kissing it gives you the gift of the gab. To reach it you lie on your back and lean out over a drop, which is a lot of effort for more talking."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "cork_annie_moore",
   "people": "irish",
   "title": "Annie Moore",
   "text": "On 1 January 1892 a Cork teenager called Annie Moore became the first immigrant to pass through Ellis Island in New York. She had sailed from Queenstown, now Cobh, in Cork Harbour. Millions left Ireland through Cobh, and a statue of Annie and her brothers stands on the quay there."
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "cork_jazz_festival",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Cork Jazz Festival",
   "text": "Every October bank holiday since 1978, Cork fills with jazz. Bands play in pubs, hotels, shops and the odd doorway, for a long weekend of trumpets and late nights. The rain joins in too, mostly on percussion."
  },
  {
   "x": 5,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "cork_macswiney",
   "title": "Terence MacSwiney",
   "text": "Terence MacSwiney, Lord Mayor of Cork, was arrested in 1920 and went on hunger strike in Brixton Prison in London. He died after 74 days. His belief that it is those who can endure the most, not those who inflict the most, who will win was quoted around the world."
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "arts_rory_gallagher",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Rory Gallagher",
   "text": "Rory Gallagher was born in Ballyshannon in 1948 and grew up in Cork, where he bought a battered 1961 Fender Stratocaster as a teenager and played it until the paint came off. With Taste, and then under his own name, he played the blues loud and honest all over the world and never once chased a hit single. He died in 1995, aged 47. Cork named a square after him and put up a sculpture of a twisted guitar. The paint still has not been put back."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "roadbowling_cork",
   "title": "Road Bowling",
   "text": "ROAD BOWLING. Cork and Armagh keep a sport that is thrown, not rolled: a 28 ounce iron bowl, underarm, along a closed country road, and the fewest throws to the finish wins. Scouts stand at the bends to mark where it lands."
  }
 ],
